{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"06","section":"03","chapter_title":"GitHub Pages Personal Website","title":"GitHub Repository Setup","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting github repository setup to GitHub Pages file tree with accessibility, privacy, and publishing checks, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the GitHub Pages file tree with accessibility, privacy, and publishing checks shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_github_pages_personal_website","cat100_supplement_github_repository_setup","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":3,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/06/02"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_6_3_github_repository_setup_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_6_3_github_repository_setup_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_6_3_github_repository_setup_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_6_3_github_repository_setup_calibration"]},"content":"# GitHub Repository Setup\n\n![GitHub Repository Setup textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/06-03-github-repository-setup.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA GitHub repository (repo) is a folder whose entire history is tracked. Setting one up correctly is what turns the loose files on your laptop into a published, recoverable, shareable project. Two setup decisions in particular have consequences that are hard to undo later: the exact name of the repository, which determines your live URL, and what you allow into version history, because anything committed once lives in the history even after you delete it.\n\nThis section walks the real commands (`git init`, `git add`, `git commit`, `git remote`, `git push`) and the two guardrails beginners most often skip: naming a user site so it serves from the clean root URL, and using a `.gitignore` so secrets never enter history in the first place. Getting these right now prevents the most painful failures in publishing (06.04) and maintenance (06.07).\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the GitHub Pages file tree with accessibility, privacy, and publishing checks shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA student wants her portfolio at the clean address `mlopez.github.io`. She creates a repo called `portfolio`, pushes everything, and enables Pages. Her site appears, but at `mlopez.github.io/portfolio/`, not the root she wanted, and worse, every absolute stylesheet path she wrote now points to the wrong place. Separately, in her rush she committed a `token.txt` file holding an API key. She deletes the file in her next commit and assumes it is gone. It is not: the key still sits in the repo's history, visible to anyone who looks, which means it must be rotated, not just deleted.\n\nBoth problems trace to a single setup moment. The repository name fixed the URL shape, and the first commit fixed what entered history. This section teaches you to make those two decisions deliberately.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nRepository setup rests on a few mechanics that beginners can learn in one sitting.\n\n**Repo name determines URL.** GitHub Pages distinguishes a user site from a project site by the repo name. A repo named exactly `username.github.io` publishes at the root, `https://username.github.io/`. Any other name publishes as a project site at a subpath, `https://username.github.io/reponame/`. The subpath is not wrong, but it changes how your asset paths must be written (the cause of the broken-CSS case above), so choose the name with the URL you want in mind.\n\n**The commit pipeline.** Git tracks changes in three stages. The working directory is your files as they sit. `git add` moves chosen files to the staging area, a holding pen for the next snapshot. `git commit` records that snapshot permanently with a message. `git push` uploads commits to GitHub. Understanding the stages explains why `git add` then `git commit` are two separate steps: staging lets you choose precisely what goes into each snapshot.\n\n**History is permanent; `.gitignore` is prevention.** A commit is a permanent record. Deleting a file later removes it from the current snapshot but not from past commits. The correct defense is to never commit secrets: a `.gitignore` file lists patterns (like `token.txt` or `.DS_Store`) that Git will refuse to stage. Prevention beats cleanup because cleaning history is hard and, for a leaked credential, insufficient (you still have to rotate the key).\n\n> **Recent evidence.** Prevention now has platform help: since March 2024, GitHub enables secret scanning push protection by default on new public repositories owned by personal accounts, so a push containing a known secret type can be blocked before it ever enters history. If a secret does reach history, the current standard cleanup tool is `git filter-repo` (the replacement GitHub recommends for the deprecated `git filter-branch`), but rotation is still mandatory either way: empirical scans of public GitHub found leaked keys are harvested within seconds of appearing (Meli et al., 2019), so a committed credential must be treated as compromised, not merely deleted.\n\nThese mechanics meet [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students) directly: committing a key is a real security exposure, and knowing that history is permanent is the difference between a recoverable mistake and a published one. [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined) applies too, because version control and not leaking credentials are baseline expectations in any technical workplace.\n\n<inline-check question=\"You commit token.txt holding an API key, then in the next commit you delete the file. Is the key now safe?\" explanation=\"A commit is a permanent record. Deleting the file in a later commit removes it from the current snapshot but leaves it readable in history. The only real fix is to rotate (revoke and reissue) the key, and the prevention is to list it in .gitignore before the first commit so it never enters history.\" options='[{\"text\":\"No: the key still lives in history, so you must rotate it, not just delete the file\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Yes: deleting the file in a new commit removes it from the repo entirely\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Yes, as long as you also run git push after deleting it\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nMaria wants her site live at https://mlopez.github.io. Follow the repository setup command by command.\n\n**Step 1. Name the repository to match the target URL.** A user site must use the exact repo name username.github.io:\n\n```text\nRepository name: mlopez.github.io   (correct)\nRepository name: portfolio          (wrong: would publish at /portfolio)\n```\n\n**Step 2. Initialize and connect the local folder.**\n\n```bash\ncd my-portfolio\ngit init\ngit add index.html about.html assets/\ngit commit -m \"Initial portfolio\"\ngit branch -M main\ngit remote add origin https://github.com/mlopez/mlopez.github.io.git\ngit push -u origin main\n```\n\nThe `git branch -M main` line is not decoration: GitHub's default branch is `main`, but `git init` on Git versions before 2.28 (or on any version without `init.defaultBranch` configured) names the first branch `master`, so without the rename the final push fails with `error: src refspec main does not match any`. Renaming the initial branch to `main` makes `git push -u origin main` match.\n\nOne real setup snag belongs on the list alongside the URL and secret snags: the first push over HTTPS prompts for credentials, and GitHub no longer accepts your account password there (password authentication for Git over HTTPS ended in August 2021). At the password prompt you supply a personal access token (PAT) generated in your GitHub settings, or you set up an SSH key and use an SSH remote instead.\n\n**Step 3. Protect secrets before the first push.** Maria notices a file token.txt in the folder. She adds a .gitignore:\n\n```text\n# .gitignore\ntoken.txt\n.DS_Store\n```\n\nIf the token had already been committed, removing it would not be enough; she would also rotate the key because it now lives in history.\n\n**Step 4. Confirm the entry file.** The root URL serves index.html in lowercase. A file named Index.HTML would return a 404, so she renames it.\n\nThe repository is now named correctly, the secret is ignored before it can leak, and the lowercase index.html guarantees the root URL resolves. Each setup choice is traceable to a specific publishing rule.\n\nOrder the setup steps so the irreversible decisions happen before the first push. The repo name and what enters history are both hard to undo, so they must be settled first.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Name the repo username.github.io so it serves from the clean root URL\",\"Add a .gitignore listing token.txt so the secret never enters history\",\"Confirm a lowercase index.html exists at the served level\",\"git init, then git add only the files you intend to publish\",\"git commit with a clear message, then git remote add origin\",\"git push to upload the first snapshot to GitHub\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nBefore your first push, run three checks in this order, because each one is hard to reverse afterward:\n\n1. **Name check:** Does the repo name produce the URL you want? Use `username.github.io` for a root user site; accept a subpath only if you have written your asset paths to suit it.\n2. **Secret check:** Is there any credential, key, token, or private path among the files? If so, add it to `.gitignore` before you stage anything. If a secret was already committed, plan to rotate it, not just delete it.\n3. **Entry-file check:** Is there a lowercase `index.html` at the level GitHub will serve? The root URL looks for `index.html`; a file named `Index.HTML` returns a 404 on case-sensitive servers.\n\nA failure here is not cosmetic. The wrong name means rebuilding URLs; a leaked secret means a real exposure; a missing index means a blank live site.\n\nWalk one repo through the three checks and predict each verdict before you reveal it. The repo is named portfolio, contains a committed config with a database password, and has an Index.HTML at the root.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Run the name, secret, and entry-file checks on this repo before the first push\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Name check first: the repo is named portfolio and the goal is the clean root URL username.github.io. Does the name produce the URL you want?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Yes, any repo name serves from the root\",\"No, portfolio publishes at the /portfolio subpath, not the root\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Only the exact string username.github.io serves from the root. portfolio becomes a project site at /portfolio/, which also changes how asset paths must be written.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Secret check next: a config file with a database password is already committed. What does the check require?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Just delete the file in the next commit\",\"Rotate the password and add the file to .gitignore, because history is permanent\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Deleting it later leaves it readable in history, so the credential must be rotated. Going forward, list it in .gitignore so it never re-enters history.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Entry-file check: Index.HTML is not lowercase index.html, so the root request can 404 on a case-sensitive server. Verdict: rename the repo (or accept the subpath and fix paths), rotate the leaked password, and rename the entry file to lowercase index.html, all before pushing.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most damaging misreading is \"I deleted the file, so the secret is gone.\" Git history keeps every committed version. Deleting a file in a new commit hides it from the current snapshot but leaves it readable in history, which is why a committed key must be rotated. Treat the first commit as a one-way door for anything sensitive.\n\nA second misreading is assuming any repo name works the same. The exact string `username.github.io` is special; everything else becomes a subpath site with different path requirements.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the rule to a clean setup. A student named Maria wants `mlopez.github.io`. She names the repo exactly that, satisfying the name check and the root-URL goal in one decision. Before staging, she scans the folder and spots `token.txt` from an earlier experiment; she writes a `.gitignore` containing `token.txt` and `.DS_Store`, satisfying the secret check before anything sensitive can enter history. She confirms the entry file is lowercase `index.html`, satisfying the entry-file check.\n\nOnly then does she run the commit pipeline: `git add index.html about.html assets/`, `git commit -m \"Initial portfolio\"`, connect the remote, and `git push`. Because she checked the three things first, the live site appears at the clean root URL, the token never reaches GitHub, and the root request resolves to her home page. The order of operations is the lesson: the checks come before the push, not after.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader can predict the consequence of each setup choice before running it. As you read the worked example, pause at the repo-name line and ask what URL it produces; pause at the `.gitignore` and ask what would have happened without it. If you can state the downstream effect of each command, you understand setup as a chain of consequences rather than a recipe to copy.\n\nA strong reader of repository setup can answer four questions:\n\n1. Given a repo name, what will the live URL be, root or subpath?\n2. What is the difference between staging and committing, and why are they separate?\n3. If a secret was committed three commits ago, what does it take to truly remove the risk?\n4. What single file prevents secrets from entering history, and how does it work?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nYour repository is strong when a reviewer cloning it finds no secrets in any commit, a sensible `.gitignore`, a clear commit message, and a lowercase `index.html` that the root URL serves. The history should read as deliberate: each commit groups related changes rather than dumping everything at once.\n\nA strong submission documents one prevention you made on purpose: \"I added token.txt to .gitignore before the first commit, because once a key is in history, deleting the file is not enough and I would have had to rotate it.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nTake the same setup skills to a different project: a small Python script you want to share rather than a website. The URL concern disappears (you may not publish Pages at all), but the history and secrets concerns are identical. You would still write a `.gitignore`, this time excluding a `config.py` with database passwords and a `__pycache__` folder, and you would still treat the first commit as permanent. The specific patterns in `.gitignore` change with the project type; the principle that history is permanent and secrets must be kept out of it never changes. Carrying that principle to a non-web project is the test that you learned version-control hygiene, not just one portfolio recipe.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the GitHub Pages file tree with accessibility, privacy, and publishing checks packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/06-03-github-repository-setup-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"GitHub Pages file tree with accessibility, privacy, and publishing checks\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"06.03\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted. A concrete current example of the bounded support move: an in-terminal assistant such as GitHub Copilot CLI (generally available as of 2026) will translate natural language into git commands, and the force-push suggestion in this section's practice set is exactly the kind you reject and log with a reason. Tool names and models churn quickly, so the documented judgment, not the tool, is the artifact.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the GitHub Pages file tree with accessibility, privacy, and publishing checks.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the GitHub Pages file tree with accessibility, privacy, and publishing checks for CAT 100 06.03. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_github_repository_setup\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace links claim, evidence, tool boundary, revision, and remaining limitation. That is what separates digital fluency from simple completion.\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_github_repository_setup\", \"cat100_supplement_github_pages_personal_website\", \"evidence_based_revision\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_3_github_repository_setup_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_github_repository_setup","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_github_repository_setup","cat100_supplement_github_pages_personal_website"],"stem":"To publish a user site at https://mlopez.github.io with GitHub Pages, what must the repository be named?","options":["portfolio","mlopez.github.io","website-final-v3","github-pages"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A GitHub user site is served from a repository named exactly username.github.io. Any other repo name produces a project site at a /repo subpath instead.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_6_3_repo_name_arbitrary","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_3_github_repository_setup_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_github_repository_setup","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_github_repository_setup"],"stem":"Your home page is in a file called Index.HTML. The published site shows a 404 at the root URL. What is the most likely cause?","options":["GitHub Pages requires the entry file to be named index.html in lowercase","The repository is too small","GitHub Pages does not support HTML files","You need to pay for hosting"],"correct_index":0,"explanation":"GitHub Pages serves index.html (lowercase) as the root document. Index.HTML is treated as a different filename, so the root URL has nothing to serve.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_6_3_filename_case_ignored","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_3_github_repository_setup_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning","cat100_supplement_github_repository_setup"],"stem":"An AI assistant tells you to run 'git push -f origin main' to fix a rejected push on your shared class repo. What is the responsible move?","options":["Run it immediately; force-push always fixes push errors","Pause: a force push can overwrite teammates' commits, so first run 'git pull' to integrate their work, then push normally","Delete the repository and start over","Ignore the error and keep editing files locally forever"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Force-pushing to a shared branch can erase others' commits. AI suggestions about destructive git commands must be judged against the cost; pulling first is the safe path.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_6_3_force_push_is_safe","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_6_3_github_repository_setup_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_github_repository_setup","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_github_repository_setup","evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"You accidentally committed a file named secrets.txt containing an API key. What should you do?","options":["Nothing; private repos can never leak","Remove the file, add it to .gitignore, commit the removal, and rotate (regenerate) the exposed key","Rename the file to notes.txt and leave the key in it","Make the repo public so more people can help"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Once a key is committed it is in history. You remove and gitignore the file and, crucially, rotate the key, because the old one must be treated as compromised.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_6_3_committed_secret_is_safe","difficulty":"medium"}]},"content_version":{"content_version":"2bce607676bfb4616152ddb79c981f21e144e5ecbd7e8caf74b12a99a60beb2b","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}